.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-or-later
.Dd November 23, 2026
.Dt NQSXMK 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqsxmk ,
.Nm nqsxchk ,
.Nm nqsxsz
.Nd the version 2 secret-key container, with an integrity value
.Sh LIBRARY
libnoqtis, \-lnoqtis
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In noqtis.h
.Ft int
.Fn nqsxsz "nqsalg alg" "size_t *n"
.Ft int
.Fo nqsxmk
.Fa "nqsalg alg"
.Fa "const uint8_t *sk"
.Fa "size_t sklen"
.Fa "uint8_t *x"
.Fa "size_t xlen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fn nqsxchk "nqsalg alg" "const uint8_t *x" "size_t xlen"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
For a signature algorithm with standard secret key
.Va S
(FIPS 204, FIPS 205, RFC 8032, or for a hybrid the ML-DSA key followed
by the 32-byte Ed25519 seed) the container is
.Bd -literal -offset indent
X = S || P || T
T = SHA3-256("noqtis-sk-2" || 0x00 || alg || 0x00 || S || P)
.Ed
.Pp
with
.Va P
the Ed25519 public key (32 bytes) for Ed25519 and the hybrids and empty
otherwise, and
.Va alg
the algorithm's name as
.Xr nqsname 3
gives it.
.Pp
.Fn nqsxsz
sets
.Fa *n
to the length of
.Fa alg Ns 's
container: that of
.Va S
plus 32, or plus 64 for Ed25519 and the hybrids.
.Fn nqsxmk
writes the container of
.Fa sk
(which must be
.Va S )
to
.Fa x ,
deriving
.Va P ;
.Fa x
may be
.Fa sk
when it has room, and
.Fa xlen
must be at least
.Fn nqsxsz Ns 's
length.
.Fn nqsxchk
checks a container: its length,
.Va T ,
and for Ed25519 and the hybrids that
.Va P
is the public key of the seed.
.Pp
.Xr nqsign 3 ,
.Fn nqsignd ,
.Xr nqsignmu 3
and the streaming signer
.Xr nqsini 3
take either
.Va S
or the container
.Va X .
With
.Va X
they sign with
.Va S
and then, after the signature is made and before it is released,
compute
.Va T
again from the key in memory and compare it with the stored
.Va T ,
and for Ed25519 and the hybrids derive the public key from the seed
again and compare it with
.Va P .
Each comparison leaves a verdict, and the signature leaves only through
the mask the verdicts make, as for the signers' own checks: a change
to any stored byte of the key, the fields that only feed the signer's
randomness (ML-DSA's K, SLH-DSA's SK.prf, the Ed25519 seed) among them,
ends in
.Er NQEFLT
with the output zero.
With
.Va S
alone signing is checked as before, without this check.
.Pp
The capsule, CMS, DSSE, card and key-statement functions that take a
secret key take either form too.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return
.Dv NQOK
on success, or:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er NQEARG
an unknown
.Fa alg ,
or a
.Dv NULL
pointer.
.It Bq Er NQEBUF
.Fn nqsxmk :
.Fa xlen
is too small.
.It Bq Er NQEDEC
.Fn nqsxmk :
.Fa sklen
is not the length of
.Va S .
.Fn nqsxchk :
the container is damaged (any changed byte), or has a length that is
neither
.Va S Ns 's
nor
.Va X Ns 's .
.It Bq Er NQENOSUP
.Fn nqsxchk :
.Fa xlen
is the length of
.Va S :
a key with no integrity value (version 1).
.El
.Pp
.Fn nqsxmk
wipes
.Fa x
when it fails.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nqssz z;
size_t xn;
uint8_t pk[1952], x[4032 + 32], sig[3309];
size_t n;
nqssize(NQMLDSA65, &z);
nqsxsz(NQMLDSA65, &xn);               /* 4064 */
nqskg(NQMLDSA65, pk, z.pk, x, z.sk);  /* S in the first z.sk bytes */
nqsxmk(NQMLDSA65, x, z.sk, x, xn);    /* in place: X */
if (nqsign(NQMLDSA65, x, xn, msg, msglen, NULL, 0, 0,
           sig, sizeof sig, &n) == NQEFLT) {
  /* the key or the computation was disturbed: nothing released */
}
nqwipe(x, sizeof x);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis-keygen 1 ,
.Xr noqtis-passwd 1 ,
.Xr noqtis 3 ,
.Xr nqsign 3 ,
.Xr nqwipe 3
.Pp
.Pa docs/spec/keyfile.md
of the source tree specifies the container and the key files that
carry it, with known answers in
.Pa tests/vectors/skx/kat.txt .
.Sh STANDARDS
SHA3-256 is that of FIPS 202.
.Va T
is noqtis's own and is not part of any standard key encoding: a
standard key is
.Va S ,
and
.Fn nqsxmk
makes the container of an imported one.
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.Va T
detects faults, not an attacker: it is a hash, not a MAC, and whoever
can write the key can write
.Va T
too.
It is a deterministic function of
.Va S
and
.Va P ,
so it reveals nothing the key does not; seen apart from the key it
would only let someone confirm a guess of the whole key.
The fault model is one fault; two coordinated faults (in both verdicts,
or in a verdict and the masking) are outside it.
